A canvas that animates itself keeps the bracket that drives it, and on fyne it
now repaints.

`#[tree.none]` says a component belongs to no family, which is what lets an
`effect` or a `timer` be placed in a drawing as readily as in a layout -- and
`component_treeless_in_canvas.sngl` is the fixture for that rule, whose own
prose says scheduling inside a drawing is what an animated canvas is made of.

It type-checked and rendered nothing. passCanvas turns a canvas's shape
children into a draw function and then cleared `Children` outright, so the
bracket went with the shapes -- and it runs at position 56, before passEffect
at 60 and passTimerPrimitive at 61, so neither ever saw one. `frame` stayed 0
and the drawing never moved, on every target with a canvas.

What a canvas keeps now is exactly the children belonging to no tree. They are
not shapes, so the draw function does not want them; their lifetime is the
canvas's, so they stay children rather than being lifted anywhere.

The failure was invisible twice over: the only harness that reads that fixture
compiles the output rather than running it, and a canvas whose contents never
change compiles perfectly.

The draw function is the other half. A member of no tree paints nothing, so
emitShape bracketed one in a save and a restore with nothing between them --
two calls per bracket, for as long as a canvas has been allowed to hold one.

**The repaint is the third half, and it is finished on fyne only.** Keeping the
bracket makes the tick run; it does not make the drawing move. Two separate
things stopped it. passCanvasReactivity walked node handlers and var handlers,
and an effect's `@mount` or a timer's `@tick` is neither -- so no redraw was
injected anywhere. And its state set was the owner's vars alone, so a canvas
drawn from a *package-level* var like `frame` here had an empty dep set and was
not collected as reactive at all, which is why a plain button did not repaint
one either. passReactivity has always patched the widgets that read a
package-level var, so that half was an inconsistency rather than a policy.

Both are fixed in the lowering, and the IR now carries `__canvasRedraw` in the
mount and in the tick on all three targets -- `sngl dump --stage lowered
--after CanvasReactivity` shows it for fyne, gtk4 and html alike. **Only fyne
emits it.** Both of the others drop a redraw that sits inside a lambda body,
which is the same blind spot `CLAUDE.md` records for the lowering passes, one
layer further out. html drops the one at the top of an effect's body too, for a
reason of its own: `translateBlockWithJC` lifts a redraw out of a handler and
resolves it against `canvasSetups`, and `emitJSFunc` -- which is what writes an
effect's body -- routes through `WalkLowered` and `EvalStmt` without that lift.
The translator's `OnDefault` answers one only for a canvas in `canvasByFunc`,
which a canvas the page rendered as markup is not in, so the statement is
returned unchanged and emitted as nothing at all.

So the fyne golden below repaints twice and the html one does not repaint at
all. That is a gap in those two emitters rather than anything left undone in
the lowering.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `(?m)^\tctx\.Save\(\)\n\tctx\.Restore\(\)$` -- a member of no tree paints nothing; it gets no bracket
deny out/none/html/index.html `(?m)^    ctx\.save\(\);\n    ctx\.restore\(\);$` -- likewise

-- app.sngl --
import d "sngl:ui/draw"
import time "sngl:time"
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        fyne
    }
}

var frame = 0

ui.window(title="drawing") {
    d.canvas(width=40px, height=40px) {
        d.circle(cx=20.0, cy=20.0, r=float(frame), style={fill=#3366FF})
        effect(@mount { frame = 4 })
        time.timer(interval=100ms, @tick { frame = frame + 1 })
    }
}
-- out/go/fyne/model.go --
